    text: "Robert found, through his own hands-on testing in GameMaker, a cleaner solution to the exact problem the earlier \"GML Visual: Set Instance Variable can't target a dynamically-created instance\" note (above) documented - and it changes that note's conclusion. The earlier note's finding that Set Instance Variable's Action Scope dropdown can't reach a variable-held instance reference was correct as far as it went, but its fix (a generic Assign Variable block with a typed dot-notation Name field, new_enemy.image_blend) turns out not to be necessary. The actual solution: drag an \"Instance - Apply To\" block right after Create Instance, and attach whatever needs the scoped target - an If/Else block, in this lesson's case - to the RIGHT of Apply To, not below it, which is what nests it inside Apply To's scope rather than just sequencing after it. Apply To's own Action Scope pop-up has an Expression field at the top, separate from its Self/Other/object list, and typing the same variable Create Instance's Target field captured (new_enemy) into that field re-points what \"Self\" means for every block nested inside. That means the actual purpose-built block - Set Instance Colour, not the more generic Set Instance Variable - works perfectly once nested inside Apply To's scope, with no generic block or typed workaround needed at all. Corrected in Lesson 5's Handout and Key, in Standards Rule 4 (the \"confirmed exception\" is now a resolved case), and in the Sidebars' Script vs. Visual Coding topic, which had built an entire GameMaker-vs-Unreal comparison around GML Visual allegedly having \"no socket\" for an instance reference - Apply To's Expression field is exactly that socket, just reached through a scope-setting block and a pop-up field rather than a draggable wire on the canvas. Both diagrams (wireframe and full sequence) were redrawn to match. Worth a general lesson for future audits: a purpose-built block's own targeting dropdown looking limited doesn't necessarily mean the block can't be used - check whether a scope-setting block like Apply To (or an engine's equivalent) changes what's available before concluding a generic/typed fallback is the only option.",
